{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you shall make an altar of incense</b> — Now the incense altar was among the inner furnishings; it would have been fitting to mention it together with the table and the menorah, since it is placed with them, and so indeed they are mentioned together in the account in the portion of Vayakhel (Exodus 37:25). But the reason for mentioning it here, after the Tabernacle and all its furnishings and the sacrifices, is that upon the completion of everything He said, 'and it shall be sanctified by My glory' (Exodus 29:43), 'and I will dwell in the midst of the children of Israel' (ibid. v. 45). He then said that there still remains an obligation upon them to make an altar of incense for burning incense to the glory of HaShem. And this is the secret that was transmitted to Moses our master, that the incense halts the plague (Shabbat 89a), for the incense operates through the attribute of strict judgment (Zohar, Vayikra 18), as it says, 'they shall place incense in Your nostril' (Deuteronomy 33:10) — from the root of 'and My anger shall burn' (ibid. 31:17). And this is what He said regarding strange incense: 'and before all the people I will be glorified' (Leviticus 10:3) — that they shall know My glory, for He does not forgive your transgression, and they shall be careful with My glory. Therefore He said here (Exodus 30:6), 'And you shall place it before the curtain that is upon the Ark of the Testimony, before the cover that is upon the Testimony, where I will meet with you there' — for what need is there to elaborate in all these words? He did not simply say, 'And you shall place it before the Ark of the Testimony in the Tent of Meeting,' as He said in the portion of Pekudei (Exodus 40:5). Rather, it is to indicate its significance:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The phrase מִן 'וְחָרָה אַפִּי' (from 'and My anger shall burn') links the word אף (nostril/anger) in Deuteronomy 33:10 back to the root חרה (burning anger) in Deuteronomy 31:17, reading the incense offered 'in Your nostril' as operating through the force of divine anger, i.e., the attribute of judgment. The final word עִנְיָנוֹ (its matter/significance) may also carry a kabbalistic sense left intentionally cryptic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:18:39.512918883Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}